      "id": 1,
      "text": "Rockford earned the nickname \"The Forest City\" in 1853 because its streets were lined with elms, cottonwoods, and maples.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://bestattractions.org/usa/illinois/rockford-il-local-pride/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Forest City sits on the Peoria, Pekin and Jacksonville Railroad, just seventeen miles from Pekin and thirteen from Havana.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://genealogytrails.com/ill/mason/1876/forest44.html",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Lake Forest was founded in 1857 as a stop for travelers heading south to Chicago, alongside Lake Forest College.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Forest%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Lake Forest earned Tree City USA status from the National Arbor Day Foundation for its long‑standing commitment to community forests.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Forest%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A red‑heeled sock from a Rockford factory inspired the world‑famous stuffed toy that families later made from damaged socks.",
